{
  "profile": "key-derivation/1",
  "description": "Deriving a CERTCRYPT composite keypair from ONE 32-byte master seed. Both components come from the same seed, domain-separated so it can safely materialize both. Without these vectors an implementation that picks a different KDF, hash or info string derives DIFFERENT keys from the same seed: every signature it makes fails verification, and nothing says why.",
  "rules": {
    "p256": "HKDF-SHA3-256, Extract with NO salt, Expand with info = \"certcrypt/composite/p256/v1\" (ASCII, no NUL) to 48 bytes. The 48 bytes are read as a big-endian integer and reduced mod n (the P-256 group order). Oversampling past the 32-byte field avoids the modular bias of reducing exactly 256 bits. A result of 0 maps to 1, so the scalar is always in [1, n-1]. SEC1 encoding: uncompressed is 65 bytes (0x04 ‖ X ‖ Y), compressed is 33 bytes (0x02/0x03 ‖ X).",
    "mldsa65": "FIPS 204 ML-DSA-65 KeyGen_internal with the 32-byte seed as xi, verbatim. No KDF, no domain separation: the seed IS the FIPS 204 input.",
    "why_both_from_one_seed": "The distinct HKDF info string keeps the classical derivation separated from the FIPS 204 one, so a single custodied seed materializes the whole composite identity without either component leaking into the other.",
    "composite_root_fingerprint": "SHA3-256(p256_public_key_sec1_UNCOMPRESSED ‖ mldsa65_public_key), classical component FIRST — the same order the composite signature uses. Uncompressed SEC1, not compressed: the compressed form is a different byte string and yields a different fingerprint, which is why both encodings are pinned above. This value is the system_root_fingerprint an implementation must recompute rather than trust, so a swapped root cannot pass as the published one."
  },
